WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.920
Healthcare is all about meeting the needs of a&nbsp;
community and data science can help meet those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.200
needs a little better. Specifically, when we&nbsp;
talk about meeting the community’s needs, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.400
do we mean? Well, there are three major categories&nbsp;
perhaps. The first one is identifying the needs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.880
Second, it is about optimizing delivery.&nbsp;
Getting those needs met quickly and efficiently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.760
And third, assessing effectiveness to see&nbsp;
how well you’re doing and see if there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.280
ways that you can change the services&nbsp;
to better meet those communities needs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.840
Let’s talk about optimizing delivery&nbsp;
and assessing effectiveness.&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.240
When it comes to optimizing delivery, there’s&nbsp;
several aspects that data science can contribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:57.600
to. The first one is, in terms of just logistics.&nbsp;
For example, coordinating the emergency department&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.840
functions, so when a person arrives in an&nbsp;
emergency room, there’s a wide range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.280
tasks to perform including things like, drawing&nbsp;
blood, getting x-rays or ultrasounds, obtaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.200
medication, and so on. Simply determining the&nbsp;
order in which these tasks should be performed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.440
not meaning always in the same order, but&nbsp;
according to what’s happening to this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.960
person, what else is going on in the ER, the&nbsp;
time of day. Determining the order and matching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:29.440
the staffing, the resources, can also make a big&nbsp;
difference. For example, some hospitals created a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.880
model to predict patient demand for each category&nbsp;
of lab tests, by time of day and day of week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.000
So, it’s an optimization model.&nbsp;
And the result is that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.400
able to reduce average patient wait time for&nbsp;
lab tests, by 75%, from one hour to 15 minutes.&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.160
Second, there’s operating room schedules. The ORs&nbsp;
in the hospital, are one of the busiest, and one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:01.040
of the most important places in the hospital. They&nbsp;
account for 60% of admissions and also 60% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.040
revenue of the hospital. But, in most hospitals,&nbsp;
the operating rooms are scheduled by phone calls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.960
or faxes, or emails. That makes scheduling&nbsp;
slow and it’s also awkward to make changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.320
For instance, when a surgeon ends up not&nbsp;
needing the room for as long as he anticipated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.520
They are actually also less likely to make&nbsp;
those changes because the process is awkward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.400
So this is an area that is ripe for tech. For&nbsp;
example, UC Health in Colorado went through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:36.560
relatively simple process of creating a smartphone&nbsp;
app, that could schedule the operating rooms. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.040
they found that surgeons increased the number of&nbsp;
scheduled blocks that they released, by nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:48.080
50%, and did so 10% sooner. And that freed up some&nbsp;
of the most valuable resources in the hospital.&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.760
One of the interesting element of&nbsp;
optimizing delivery is discharge planning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.960
When a person has received the help they needed&nbsp;
at a hospital and it’s time for them to leave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.040
Simple problems like verifying their insurance,&nbsp;
or even having a ride home can slow things down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.360
Hospitals like MedStar Georgetown University&nbsp;
Hospital in Washington, D.C., they developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.680
models that helped them predict bottlenecks&nbsp;
in discharge as soon as the patient checks in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.160
This allowed to increase their daily discharge&nbsp;
volume, by over 20% and reduce the length of stay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.840
that is the time spent just waiting to get out,&nbsp;
by half a day. Again, an immense improvement in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:32.320
efficiency, that allows the hospital to devote&nbsp;
their resource to other people who need it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.280
as opposed to people who are just&nbsp;
waiting to get out of the building.&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.000
There is also the matter of assessing&nbsp;
effectiveness. How well is the hospital performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.120
its functions and can it do so in a way that’s&nbsp;
both, more time effective and more cost effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.680
Well, there’s several elements to this. One of&nbsp;
them, for instance, is reducing readmission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.720
Hospital visits are expensive and while it&nbsp;
saves money to not keep people there any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.520
longer than they need, it really is problematic&nbsp;
and much more expensive, to send somebody out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.120
too soon or with inadequate support. So they end&nbsp;
up having to come back again for the same issue.&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.840
There’s also reducing health&nbsp;
problems for premature babies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.920
For instance, at some hospitals, researchers&nbsp;
using high-frequency physiological data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.480
such as heart rate and respiration rate, to&nbsp;
create algorithms that can predict when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.480
premature baby is at risk of infection and other&nbsp;
health complications. Now what’s really amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.360
about this, is these algorithms are able to&nbsp;
identify the tell-tale signs of infection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.200
in changes in their heart rate, up 24 hours&nbsp;
before the infection takes hold. This allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.760
the hospital time to take preventive action&nbsp;
and potentially save the life of the newborn.&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.880
There’s also the matter of reducing travel time&nbsp;
for healthcare. Depending on where you live, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.640
may have to travel for hours to see, for example,&nbsp;
a specialist in a rare form of cancer, or even for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:04.880
something as common as reproductive health. Data&nbsp;
science algorithms can help hospitals and clinics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.760
identity which locations are at greatest need&nbsp;
for specific services, and also determine the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.520
schedules that would best match the availability&nbsp;
of the people who need those services.&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.760
You can also improve effectiveness by looking&nbsp;
at something like, how well are your program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.440
reducing the waiting time to see healthcare&nbsp;
professionals? Doctor’s offices are notorious for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.720
long wait times. It would be nice if the patient&nbsp;
can come in and out of there in 15 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.840
The system can use the information that you had&nbsp;
given them previously, that they were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:40.640
gather information passively, that this sharing&nbsp;
between organizations was seamless so that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.440
didn’t have to provide the same information over&nbsp;
and over and over again, and that scheduling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:52.320
could be better coordinated so that there&nbsp;
was less time waiting and lagging between.&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.200
All of these things taken together, the ability&nbsp;
to identify the places of greatest need,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:01.680
the ability to coordinate care effectively,&nbsp;
the ability to assess effectiveness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.760
all of these, are ways that data science can&nbsp;
help make the provision of healthcare more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:16.560
effective and really, more beneficial&nbsp;
to the communities that they serve.

